\description{
Creates numerical integration settings for the product and best-p-value
selection likelihoods used by \code{\link[=bselmodel]{bselmodel()}}, \code{\link[=bselmodel.mv]{bselmodel.mv()}}, \code{\link[=RoBMA]{RoBMA()}}, and
\code{\link[=RoBMA.mv]{RoBMA.mv()}}. Covariance factors use deterministic sequences of
Gauss-Hermite rules. Certified factors of structural rank one through eight
use deterministic interval refinement for rank-one product fallbacks and
randomized quasi-Monte Carlo for other unresolved factor integrals.
General covariance blocks with
product weights first try same-diagonal covariance approximations with a
common Gaussian factor and independent group factors. Monotone weights
permit covariance bounds on the normalizer. Two-sided and other nonmonotone
weights require a covariance-perturbation bound; agreement of auxiliary
normalizers alone is insufficient. The absolute bound uses the total
variation of the step weights. Strictly positive weights also permit a
relative Gaussian covariance bound, including reconstructed diagonal and
factor rounding, which remains useful for tiny normalizers. Their original
full covariance remains authoritative.
The covariance error bound, estimated
quadrature error, and a bound on Gaussian mass outside the quadrature nodes
share one numerical error budget. Interior quadrature error is estimated,
so this is not a rigorous probability interval. Unsupported or unresolved
cases use randomized quasi-Monte Carlo in the dense conditional-normal
representation.
Under product weights, a deterministic rung of the factor or cluster
quadrature ladder is accepted by a shift-amplitude certificate. Shifting the
nodes of one latent axis by a fraction of the node gap and reweighting leaves
the integral unchanged, so the change of the rule under half and quarter gap
shifts of each axis measures its aliasing error. The rung's successive
changes must be within \code{relative_tolerance}, the summed shift amplitudes must
not exceed half of it, and twice their sum, together with the exterior
Gaussian mass, must fit within it. A rung must also resolve the selection
edges: the edge width (conditional residual SD divided by the absolute
loading) is at least 0.15 times the node gap. When no rung is accepted,
rank-one clusters, tensors of rank at most two and depth-one forests try the
average of the finest rung over four shifts per axis before sparse grids and
the randomized fallback. That average asks the edges for at least one eighth
of the node gap, with a separate penalty for each axis's row count, and is used only when
\code{relative_tolerance} is at least 0.005, the tolerance at which its checks
were calibrated. For product weights with selection transitions, the ladder
and this refinement tier use the smaller of \code{relative_tolerance} and 0.005
for their acceptance checks: a loose request must not relax the calibration
of these deterministic rules. After these checks decline, rank-one product
integrals refine bounds over the complete Gaussian line, including both tails.
Exact row groups and nonnegative excess weights give interval extrema; the
returned midpoint must satisfy the requested relative tolerance against both
bounds. Floating arithmetic uses an explicit working-precision allowance,
rather than a universal guarantee for library probability evaluations.
Insufficient work or unresolved arithmetic is rejected without a QMC retry.
Other randomized fallbacks retain the requested tolerance and their empirical
diagnostics. The best-p-value rule and the other deterministic routes
instead require that the node gap across an interior transition not exceed
its conditional residual SD divided by the absolute loading; coupled axes use
conservative central node spacing. Unresolved candidates refine or use the
existing fallback; the numerical settings and likelihood target are
unchanged. These checks are heuristics, not a bound on the total integration
error, including reciprocal normalizers.
Each fit or post-fit request uses a fixed integration design, so repeated
likelihood evaluations are deterministic. One-observation blocks are evaluated
analytically.

Post-fit dense two-bin product normalizers can use a bounded interpolation
of the standard-normal CDF. Its deterministic error is included in the same
numerical error budget, separately from Monte Carlo standard error. Other
weights, arithmetic outside its supported range, or insufficient error
budgets retain the ordinary scalar primitive. An unresolved interpolation
attempt retries the ordinary covariance-envelope calculation before QMC.
Fitting and random generation retain their ordinary scalar primitive.

The numerical defaults are package operating settings, not statistical
estimands or literature-mandated constants. \code{points_per_scramble},
\code{max_points_per_scramble}, and \code{scrambles} set the fixed computational
design, while
\code{relative_tolerance} sets its diagnostic acceptance rule. Increasing a
budget changes only the numerical evaluation of the same likelihood target.

